\begin_layout Title
Public Goods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
October 1, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Environments with public goods provide a multitude of experimental opportunities
 for researchers interested in the inherent theoretical challenges.
 Environments with public goods present serious problems since many factors
 interact with each other in unknown ways.
 Some of the central questions about the functions of society are raised
 due to the presence of public goods.
 Economists, sociologists, and political scientists have all attempted to
 answer a fundamental quandry concerning the nature of human interaction:
 are people selfish or cooperative? Research on the voluntary provision
 of public goods has yet to answer this simple question, but it does provide
 the basis for conducting unique and thought provoking experiments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Some of the major findings associated with voluntary contribution mechansims
 have been: subjects contribute to the public good in one shot trials; contribut
ions decline with repetition; face to face communication or outright punishment
 improves the rate of contribution.
 Fehr and Gatcher (2000) provide evidence that the existence of punishment
 opportunities causes a large rise in average contribution.
 In fact, when a specific group of cohorts interacts for multiple periods,
 full cooperation emerges as the dominant behavior standard.
 Still, other studies have found that people even cooperate under less stringent
 conditions.
 For example, Andreoni (1995) finds that putting a positive frame on the
 public goods game results in an increase in the contribution level.
 Andreoni postulates that people may be motivated by the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

warm glow
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of altruism.
 However, Ashley, Ball, and Eckel (2010) help to distinguish alternative
 motives for giving, including altruism, warm glow, reciprocity, and inequality
 aversion, using data from two previous public goods experiments.
 They conclude that while individuals may contribute to the public good,
 they will adjust their contributions downward if their previous contribution
 was above the group average.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Despite all of these findings, there are still many issues that still need
 to be addressed.
 Which mechanism provides the most effective way to increase voluntary contribut
ions? For instance, which method leads to faster convergence towards full
 cooperation, communication between people or the possible threat of punishment?
 Even though punishment increases voluntary contributions, it seems impractical
 to apply this method to the real world.
 It seems unreasonable to constantly threaten and coerce people to contribute
 when they have no desire to do so.
 Moreover, I do not find the results in Ashley, Ball, and Eckel (2010) incredibl
y revealing.
 In my opinion, I believe people will contribute more when there is a possibilit
y they can receive recognition or positive feedback for their good deeds.
 Just a simple display of gratitude or praise could inspire people to contribute
 more than what they originally intended.
 It is not the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

warm glow
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of doing good deeds that motivates people, but rather the desire to be
 recognized and appreciated.
\end_layout

